.SH NAME
niac-analyze-walk - Analyze an SNMP walk file: device, interfaces, and LLDP/CDP neighbors


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBniac analyze-walk  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Analyze an SNMP walk file and extract device identity, the interface
inventory, and LLDP/CDP neighbor adjacencies.

.PP
The tool parses these standard SNMP MIBs:
  • SNMPv2-MIB        (system identity)
  • IF-MIB + ifXTable (interfaces: index, name, type, speed, status, MAC)
  • LLDP-MIB          (LLDP neighbors)
  • CISCO-CDP-MIB     (CDP neighbors)


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--graphviz\fP=""
	Write Graphviz (DOT) neighbor graph to file (use '-' for stdout)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for analyze-walk

.PP
\fB--output\fP="yaml"
	Output format (yaml, json, text)

.PP
\fB--show-neighbors\fP[=false]
	Show neighbor relationships only


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--storage-keep\fP=500
	Run history records to keep, pruned oldest first on start (0 keeps every run)

.PP
\fB--storage-path\fP=""
	Path to NIAC run history database (default: ~/.niac/niac.db)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Analyze and output as YAML
  niac analyze-walk device.walk

  # Output as JSON
  niac analyze-walk --output json device.walk

  # Show only neighbor relationships
  niac analyze-walk --show-neighbors device.walk

  # Write a Graphviz (DOT) neighbor graph
  niac analyze-walk --graphviz topology.dot device.walk
.EE


